#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4435e346-d6ad-58cd-97cb-bbd45e121759" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "12811582-7156-5b84-9ba9-5545f87894f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3993" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "At first, it may not look like we have two factors. Actually, we do, but one of them is implicit. The trick is to write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\ln{x} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing this trick, we can now use integration by parts. We let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the by-parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+C \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096398 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31a28451-005e-50dc-a7cf-5b666b1d7735" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "c7373a16-c90a-5875-8b17-8724542b35ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-493" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{e} 4x\\ln\\left(x^3\\right) \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and $v',$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot (2{x}^{2}){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}(2{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{(e)}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})-2{(1)}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{3})]-6{\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}-6(\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}-3{x}^{2}{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}-3({e}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096399 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "672d52f6-89d8-550d-863b-7c5157d59ca3" :question/math-academy-id "e-494" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\log_4{x}}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{4}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡x(-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,{log}_{4}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(-\\,\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula $\\log_4{x}=\\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{\\ln{4}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c8a9b38-30a3-52b1-9fff-2a4d191e1c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300057} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0aee70-f173-5908-827f-5f89cb4c0afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300058} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8e01d5-889e-5b4a-9acb-3c0c035897a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300059} {:answer/id #uuid "736ab1b5-d37e-536f-886c-19bb16961a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300060} {:answer/id #uuid "d3877c7f-5c76-5950-9f56-cf76e73251f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3877c7f-5c76-5950-9f56-cf76e73251f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300061} :answer-field/id #uuid "12cfabff-92bf-56e2-9172-fe6f118c2de8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e6583e6-92e6-5c07-b4f7-1730072676de" :question/math-academy-id "q-114553" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4435e346-d6ad-58cd-97cb-bbd45e121759" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c0aef57-abe2-5e9b-8ffd-b4ac250e8856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300085} {:answer/id #uuid "9fec5b92-742f-5131-b6ee-8697e0573919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300086} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4d5c0b-b085-55f3-bba7-69a470201edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-\\frac{x}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300087} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf8ad37-97c5-52c4-9f50-a7d3b497740a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300088} {:answer/id #uuid "260a2b08-273c-5530-b4b4-c3d9f234169d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "260a2b08-273c-5530-b4b4-c3d9f234169d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300089} :answer-field/id #uuid "507e7950-22fa-5522-8d6e-e608954b5950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df4a8ff9-b6f1-509e-8da7-ab67f79ced45" :question/math-academy-id "q-114579" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31a28451-005e-50dc-a7cf-5b666b1d7735" :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058af3d2-4de9-51f1-b45e-c4f6450388df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-7x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300106} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb1ad74-0ddf-591a-a83b-388cef01dd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-7x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300107} {:answer/id #uuid "d79fb862-748b-5e76-ab02-bc440f18ad12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+7x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300108} {:answer/id #uuid "caaad1f2-f17b-50eb-b0e1-dddf4b14cf61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{x}{4}-7{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300109} {:answer/id #uuid "af3459a1-862b-5808-a104-ece7d60650b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-7{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb1ad74-0ddf-591a-a83b-388cef01dd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-7x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300107} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e0d112d-a83b-571f-8593-0c01ad5a42de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "252da65e-a015-52db-8aca-5c1050a5b779" :question/math-academy-id "q-12573" :question/problem "$\\int (x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x+7\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int (x+7)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+7x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+7x)-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+7x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\text{d}x-7\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-7x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant."}]}
